usage: jit [--legacy] [-O0|-O2] [--emit tokens|ast|ir|infix|asm|hex|c|wasm] [--diff VAR] [--backend interp|x86|gcc|wasm] [--bench [N]] [--help] [--version] '<expr>' [name=value ...]

With no expression on a terminal, jit starts an interactive prompt.

options (one example each):
  --backend interp|x86|gcc|wasm  how the expression runs (default x86; gcc with --legacy;
                             wasm only emits a module: use --emit wasm)
      jit --backend gcc 'sin(x) * 2' x=0.5
  -O0 | -O2                  optimizer level (default -O2)
      jit -O0 '2 * 3 + x' x=1
  --emit STAGE               print a stage instead of running; STAGE is
                             tokens, ast, ir, infix, asm, hex (x86 backend), c (gcc backend)
                             or wasm (a WebAssembly module, hex)
      jit --emit ir 'x * 1 + 0'
      jit --emit wasm 'x + 1'
  --diff VAR                 differentiate by VAR: prints the derivative as an expression
                             (or its value, given name=value); kinks show as step(...),
                             which is printed but not parseable
      jit --diff x 'x^3 + sin(x)*x'
      jit --diff x 'sigmoid(3*x)' x=0.2
      jit --backend gcc --emit c 'sigmoid(x) * 2'
  --bench [N]                time compile + N evaluations (default 1000000) on every
                             backend: interp, x86 -O0, x86 -O2, gcc
      jit --bench 100000 'sin(x) * 2'
  --legacy                   the original assignment: sigmoid of the result, x/0 = 0
      jit --legacy '10 / 4'
  --help                     show this text
      jit --help
  --version                  show the version
      jit --version

prompt commands: :q quits, :emit STAGE turns a stage dump on or off
